  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and possible thunderstorms return\nSunday.\n\nFair weather is expected through this evening. Low overcast\nconditions are expected to develop late this evening and through\nthe overnight as moisture advection develops atop a shallow cool\nlayer on SE winds. By daybreak, expect showers to move into\nwestern Maryland and track southeast across the area. A second\nround of afternoon showers is expected to move in from the\nAppalachian region and southern Pennsylvania. A few\nthunderstorms are possible along Interstate 64 in central\nVirginia, but severe wx appears unlikely. Showers will continue\ninto the evening along a frontal zone before dissipating or\nshifting south late Sunday night. \n\nLight rain or off and on showers will persist through Tue \nmorning, particularly along and west of the Blue Ridge Mountains\nin areas of onshore flow. Expect a noticeable cooling trend \nbeginning Monday in the wake of the cold front late Sunday \nnight. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain showers & cooler temperatures persist into \nmidweek ahead of drier conditions next weekend.\n\nMid-level moisture overhead will coincide with an area of low \npressure just off the East Coast tracking north on Wednesday. Weak \ninstability at the surface will suppress thunderstorm activity for \nthe most part, but expect rain showers to continue through Wednesday \nand Thursday as shortwave disturbances continue to track across\ntShowers and possible thunderstorms return Sunday.he Mid-\nAtlantic. High temperatures continue to range in the 60s as \nthese disturbances move through. \n\nAs the coastal low skirts the Mid-Atlantic coastline, winds will \nbe elevated Wednesday and Thursday with wind gusts between 20-30 MPH \npossible, especially along the Chesapeake Bay. Winds could \npotentially be higher over the waters, and this onshore flow \ncombined with stronger tidal influence could increase coastal flood \nrisk during this period. Refer to the coastal flooding section below \nfor more details. \n\nAs this system tracks north towards the end of the week, winds along \nthe coastline should gradually decrease. Mid-level dry air moves in \noverhead from the Midwest, which should lead to diminishing rainfall \npotential heading into the weekend.",
